  "nameStory": "This bolete earned its straightforward name from its most striking feature - a vibrant red-orange cap that looks like someone dipped it in rusty paint. The 'scaber stalk' part refers to the rough, scaly texture of its stem, which feels like sandpaper when you run your finger along it.",
  "description": "The Red-capped Scaber Stalk is a chunky, sturdy bolete mushroom that's hard to miss in the forest. Its bright reddish-orange cap sits atop a thick, pale stem covered in dark, rough scales that give it a distinctly bumpy texture. Instead of gills, it has tiny pores underneath the cap that release spores. These mushrooms can grow quite large, making them one of the more substantial boletes you'll encounter in New England woods.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Vibrant red to orange-red color, often fading to yellow-orange with age; feels dry and slightly velvety to the touch",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pores",
      "description": "Tiny white to cream-colored pores under the cap instead of gills; may bruise darker when pressed",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Thick, whitish stem covered in dark brown to black rough scales that feel like coarse sandpaper",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bruising",
      "description": "Flesh may darken to blue-black or gray when cut or bruised, especially in the stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Brown spore print when cap is placed gill-side down on white paper overnight",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for these mushrooms in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, often growing near birch, aspen, and oak trees. They form beneficial partnerships with tree roots and prefer well-drained forest soils with good leaf litter. You'll typically find them scattered individually rather than in large clusters.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Not typically fruiting yet, though you might find old specimens from previous seasons decomposing on the forest floor.",
    "summer": "Prime season begins in mid to late summer, especially after warm rains when fresh specimens emerge with bright, vibrant caps.",
    "fall": "Peak fruiting continues through early fall, with many mature specimens showing the characteristic faded cap colors.",
    "winter": "Fruiting season ends, but you may spot dried or frozen remnants that can still help with winter mushroom study."
  },
  "funFact": "This mushroom's flesh can turn a dramatic blue-black color when you cut it, like a natural mood ring that reacts to being disturbed - this color change happens in just minutes and is completely harmless!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other orange-capped Leccinum boletes like the Orange Birch Bolete, but the Red-capped Scaber Stalk typically has a more intensely red cap color. Remember: 'Red cap, rough stem' - if both features match, you're likely looking at this species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "North American specimens identified as this species may actually represent different species than their European counterparts, as mycologists are still sorting out the true relationships within this complex group of similar-looking boletes.",
  "compareHints": "Among its Leccinum relatives, this species stands out for its intensely red-orange cap color - the Orange Birch Bolete is more yellow-orange, while the Brown Birch-bolete is brownish. The Ghost Bolete is much paler overall, making the Red-capped Scaber Stalk the most vibrantly colored of the group.",
